From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 01:01:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally account short renders'
 stage time by sampling

With g16_fs_short a render that asks for no timestamps writes no FS
start and end stamps, and with g16_ta_short no TA end stamp. Retirement
charges the client's fdinfo vertex and fragment time from those stamps,
so with the words on that time stopped advancing for nearly every
render, and the stage statistics lost those renders as well.

Add g16_short_acct (runtime, default 0). With N above 0 and a short
word on, about one render in N of a queue that would be published short
keeps both long forms, at a jittered interval of N - N/4 to N + N/4 so
that a workload with a period cannot align with it. Its TA and FS
spans, measured by its own stamps as before, update that queue's moving
averages, each new sample weighing 1/8. Every short render of the queue
is credited those averages for the stage it lacks, in the client's
fdinfo engine time and in a new short_acct: statistics line, from the
queue's first retired sample on. The stage: line keeps measured spans
only.

The credit is an estimate. For a steady workload the client's total
converges to its measured total; after a change it lags by about eight
samples; before the queue's first sample retires it is zero. Renders
with timestamp outputs are measured exactly and do not feed the
average.

With g16_short_acct=0, the default, or with both short words off,
nothing changes.

Assisted-by: LLM
